# Minimalist 'Zen' Planner Design

This proposal focuses on a **low-friction, high-clarity** system for managing personal and academic life without visual clutter or a steep learning curve.

## The "Zen Timeline" Concept

Instead of a complex calendar grid, we use a single vertical **Daily Flow** timeline.

### 1. Unified Daily Flow
- **Locked Items**: Classes and deadlines (synced from Canvas/NUSMods) appear as fixed blocks.
- **Fluid Items**: Personal to-dos are "vessels" that the user can drag-and-drop into open time slots.
- **Visual hierarchy**: Academic items have subtle borders; personal tasks are clean, solid-colored cards.

### 2. "The Scratchpad" (Quick Capture)
- A simple, translucent text area at the top of the planner.
- **Usage**: Type anything here (e.g., "Review CS2103 notes"). 
- **Action**: Pressing 'Enter' converts it into a "floating task" badge that sits in an "Unscheduled" area, ready to be dragged onto the timeline.

### 3. Minimalist Interactions
- **One-Click Planning**: Click an empty hour on the timeline to instantly open a small, focused input field.
- **Micro-Progress**: Hovering over a task shows a simple "Done" checkmark or "Reschedule" icon. No hidden menus.
- **Visual Breath**: Plenty of whitespace between hours to reduce the feeling of being "overwhelmed" by a packed schedule.

## Suggested Components

### [NEW] `DailyTimeline.tsx`
A clean vertical list using CSS Grid for time slots.
- **Minimal Grid**: Only show hour markers (8 AM, 9 AM, etc.).
- **Smart Snap**: Tasks snap to 15-minute increments when dragged.

### [NEW] `ZenCapture.tsx`
A single line input that uses basic NLP (e.g., "at 2pm") to auto-position tasks if specified, otherwise keeps them in the "vortex" (inbox).

## Why this works for you:
- **No Learning Curve**: If you can drag a card, you can use it.
- **Zero Clutter**: Items only appear when they are relevant. Past events dim out; future events stay clean and simple.
- **Clean Dev**: Backend remains the same; we simply change the frontend projection to be more focused.

---

> [!TIP]
> Focus on "High Contrast, Low Density". Use a monochrome palette with a single accent color for "active" items to keep the UI calm.
